    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Composite Function Using a Standard Maclaurin Expansion",
    "problem": "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$",
    "worked_solution": "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $\\sqrt{x}$ in the above expansion and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x} & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{4}}{4!}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{6}}{6!}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{720}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
